# Trivy ignore file — reviewed, accepted risks ONLY.
#
# POLICY (unchanged since ADR 0004): an entry here requires a written
# justification of why the finding does not apply, or why the risk is
# deliberately accepted, plus a review date. "We will fix it later" is not a
# justification. A transport error (e.g. an HTTP 429 from a package registry) is
# NOT a finding and must never be silenced here — fix the scan instead.
#
# ID FORMAT: Trivy reports these checks WITHOUT the `AVD-` prefix (the log line
# reads `[CRITICAL] AWS-0104`). `AVD-AWS-0104` is registered as an alias. Both
# spellings are listed for each entry so the file keeps working whichever form a
# future Trivy release emits.
#
# REVIEW DATE: 2026-02-28 (6 months).
#
# =============================================================================
# AWS-0107 — ingress from the public internet (ports 80 and 443 only)
# =============================================================================
# This is a PUBLIC WEB APPLICATION. nginx terminates traffic on 80/443 and the
# pipeline's own verify stage health-checks the service over the public IP.
# Those ports being world-reachable is the product requirement; the check cannot
# distinguish an intentionally public web port from an accidentally exposed
# admin port.
#
# WHAT WAS FIXED RATHER THAN IGNORED: this same check also fired on SSH (port
# 22), which was open to 0.0.0.0/0. That was a REAL misconfiguration and it is
# fixed in code — port 22 is now restricted to GitHub's published Actions runner
# CIDR ranges (see the `app_ssh` rule and the `github_meta` data source in
# infra/network.tf). Only the deliberately public web ports remain.
#
# Residual risk accepted: the application is the security boundary on 80/443.
# That boundary is exercised by the JWT authentication suite, the Semgrep SAST
# gate, and the smoke checks (unauthenticated -> 401, forged token -> 401).
#
# Revisit if: a load balancer or CDN is introduced (terminate there and close
# the instance ports), or the service stops being publicly reachable.
AWS-0107
AVD-AWS-0107

# =============================================================================
# AWS-0104 — unrestricted egress to any IP address
# =============================================================================
# Fires on the `app_egress` rule in infra/network.tf.
#
# WHY THIS CANNOT BE FIXED BY NARROWING PORTS. The check's own Rego tests the
# CIDR and nothing else:
#
#     deny contains res if {
#         some rule in input.aws.ec2.securitygroups[_].egressrules
#         some block in rule.cidrs
#         net.cidr_allows_all_ips(block.value)
#     }
#
# There is no port predicate. Restricting egress to 443/80/53/5432 would still
# carry cidr_ipv4 = "0.0.0.0/0" and would fail identically — a change that looks
# like a fix, is not one, and costs a CI round trip. (Verified against the check
# source before making the call, rather than after.)
#
# WHY THE CIDR STAYS OPEN. Clearing the check requires enumerating outbound
# destination CIDRs, and this host legitimately needs: Ubuntu apt mirrors (a
# rotating CDN estate), ghcr.io for the application image, the CloudWatch Logs
# and SSM endpoints, and RDS. Pinning that set would be guesswork that breaks
# the configure stage the first time a mirror moves — the exact "hardening that
# locks out the configure stage" failure mode.
#
# This is also consistent with the tier: egress restriction is Tier-3 work that
# belongs behind a NAT gateway with VPC endpoints for the AWS services, which a
# single-instance deployment in the default VPC does not have.
#
# Residual risk accepted: an attacker who achieves code execution on the
# instance can reach arbitrary outbound hosts. Compensating controls in place:
# IMDSv2 is required (blocking the SSRF-to-credential-theft path), the instance
# profile is least-privilege, inbound SSH is restricted to CI runners, and the
# database is reachable only from the app security group.
#
# Revisit if: the deployment moves behind a NAT gateway, VPC endpoints are added
# for S3/CloudWatch/SSM/ECR, or the workload begins handling real customer data
# — at which point egress filtering becomes worth its operational cost.
AWS-0104
AVD-AWS-0104
